      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm also trying to figure out what usually causes sudden, unpredictable braking inconsistencies or \"spongy\" pedal feel during these high-heat cycles. Is that usually a failure of the material itself, or is it more about how the heat interacts with the hydraulic components? Like, do things just start acting up when they hit a certain threshold?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sudden, unpredictable braking inconsistencies or \"spongy\" pedal feel during high-heat cycles can be attributed to both the material properties and the interaction with hydraulic components. When brake pads reach a certain temperature threshold, they can start to glaze, which reduces their effectiveness and can cause a spongy pedal feel. Additionally, the heat can cause the hydraulic fluid to expand, leading to a decrease in hydraulic pressure and a less responsive pedal feel. This is often exacerbated by the thermal expansion of the brake lines and calipers, which can lead to a loss of brake fluid and a decrease in braking performance. So, while the material itself can contribute to the issue, the interaction with the hydraulic components is also a significant factor. It's a delicate balance, and when the heat gets too high, it can cause a cascade of issues that lead to inconsistent braking performance.",
      "source": "target",
