      "content": "Modeling sudden, erratic failures in a simulation, especially those that occur without a clear mechanical precursor, can be quite challenging. However, you can approach this by incorporating stochastic elements into your model to account for the unpredictable nature of these failures. One way to do this is by using a probabilistic failure model, such as a Monte Carlo simulation, which can simulate a wide range of scenarios and account for the random nature of these failures. You can also use a thermal model to simulate the heat buildup in the brake system and its impact on the materials and hydraulic components. By combining these models, you can simulate the system's behavior under different conditions and identify the factors that contribute to the sudden, erratic failures. It's important to remember that these models are only as good as the data and assumptions they're based on, so it's crucial to validate your model with real-world data whenever possible.",
